  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Wednesday/...\nIssued 1253 AM MDT WED OCT 28 2026\nAs the shortwave in the short-term period departs, the main low will \nbegin moving its way through our area. This trough will have a good \npull of Pacific moisture ahead of it, with precipitable water values \nincreasing to around the 90th percentile in southwest Idaho. The \nbetter juxtaposition of forcing and moisture will fall over \nsouthwest Idaho, which will lead to increasing precipitation chances \nthrough the day Saturday, reaching 50-80% Saturday evening and night \nas a cold front crosses the area. That being said, an umbrella may \nbe a fine addition to your Halloween costume. Snow levels will \ninitially begin at 6-8 kft MSL, lowering to 4.5-6.5 kft MSL by \nSunday afternoon. These lowering snow levels will coincide with \ndecreasing precipitation chances as conditions dry out behind the \nfront. Overall, the bulk of any snow accumulation will remain above \n6.5 kft MSL, with generally 2-5 inches of accumulation (Locally up \nto 7 on the highest peaks). The lowering snow levels could allow for \nsnow or wintry mix as low as 4.5 kft Sunday, although little \naccumulation is anticipated as precipitation tapers off behind the \ndeparting system. Ridging will build in behind the departing system, \nallowing for dry conditions Monday afternoon through at least mid-\nweek. Temperatures will be seasonable or below normal throughout the \nperiod.",
